-- title:
--   Lattice membership as integrality of all coordinates
-- statement:
--   Let $\mathbb{Z}^d$ (`lattice d`) denote the standard integer lattice viewed as a subset of the Euclidean space $\mathbb{R}^d$. For $x \in \mathbb{R}^d$,
--   $$x \in \mathbb{Z}^d \iff \text{for every index } i, \text{ there is } n \in \mathbb{Z} \text{ with } x_i = n ,$$
--   i.e. exactly when every coordinate of $x$ is an integer.
--
--   This is the coordinatewise characterisation of the lattice, and it is the bridge between the geometric description of the lattice as a subset of $\mathbb{R}^d$ and the arithmetic one that counting arguments use: bounds on the number of lattice points in a region are proved by bounding the number of admissible integer values of each coordinate, which requires the membership condition in this form.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The right-hand side quantifies over an existential integer $n$ per coordinate rather than asserting that the coordinate lies in the range of the coercion $\mathbb{Z} \to \mathbb{R}$; the two are the same statement, phrased so that the witness is directly available.
